{"name":"Delft Blue","hex":"#3311ee","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"delft","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of Delft (“style of earthenware”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A delf; a mine, quarry, pit or ditch."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/delft"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Delftware","blocks":[{"text":"Delftware or Delft pottery, also known as Delft Blue or as delf, is a general term now used for Dutch tin-glazed earthenware, a form of faience. Most of it is blue and white pottery, and the city of Delft in the Netherlands was the major centre of production, but the term covers wares with other colours, and made elsewhere. It is also used for similar pottery, English delftware."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Vase%2C_c._1680%2C_Delft%2C_Netherlands%2C_tin-glazed_earthenware_-_Art_Institute_of_Chicago_-_DSC09979.JPG/330px-Vase%2C_c._1680%2C_Delft%2C_Netherlands%2C_tin-glazed_earthenware_-_Art_Institute_of_Chicago_-_DSC09979.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delftware"},{"title":"Delft","blocks":[{"text":"Delft is a city and municipality in the province of South Holland, Netherlands. It is located between Rotterdam, to the southeast, and The Hague, to the northwest. Together with them, it is a part of both the Rotterdam–The Hague metropolitan area and the Randstad."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Delft_Blick_von_der_Nieuwe_Kerk_auf_die_Oude_Kerk_1.jpg/330px-Delft_Blick_von_der_Nieuwe_Kerk_auf_die_Oude_Kerk_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delft"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Delftware_display.JPG/500px-Delftware_display.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Delftware_display.JPG/1280px-Delftware_display.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Delftware_display.JPG/500px-Delftware_display.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Window display of Delftware in the market place, Delft","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADelftware_display.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Willem_Jansz._Verstraeten_-_Scotel_met_onbekende_wapen_BK-NM-14179.jpg/500px-Willem_Jansz._Verstraeten_